00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:04.840
There was a big race, it was Sebring, Florida, the 12-hour international endurance race.

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:07.640
It was a manufacturer's championship, it was a very big thing,

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:11.060
and I was racing a prototype Porsche 906 long-nosed car.

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:16.360
And I asked, you know, way prior, if I could get away from the show for this,

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:18.860
because it's a big race for me, knowing everybody said,

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:22.520
oh, Dick, no, you can't race, it was so valuable, what if you got hurt?

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:23.660
CBS said, go ahead and race.

00:42:23.660 --> 00:42:24.440
Go ahead and do it.

00:42:24.580 --> 00:42:28.040
And I said to my wife, I said, honey, do you mind if I go race?

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:28.980
She said, no, it's fine.

00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:29.920
I said, Tom, you're...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:30.960
No, go ahead and do it.

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:33.240
So that sort of hurt my feelings,

00:42:33.260 --> 00:42:35.500
and then nobody asked me to insure myself either.

00:42:36.260 --> 00:42:39.040
And so they got the idea of Dan coming in.

00:42:39.620 --> 00:42:42.340
So Dan became my straight man.

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:43.360
And we got fired.

00:42:43.580 --> 00:42:45.200
As soon as I'm gone, you get fired.

00:42:45.670 --> 00:42:47.170
Well, I talked to you,

00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:48.910
and I said we're having problems on this show.

00:42:48.990 --> 00:42:50.670
I don't even remember how you found out.

00:42:50.710 --> 00:42:51.370
I remember now.

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:54.410
I was up in San Francisco,

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:57.160
because the Smothers Brothers Comedy Hour

00:42:57.160 --> 00:42:58.920
had just been picked up for the fourth season.

00:42:59.160 --> 00:42:59.980
I was in San Francisco.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:08.300
doing a survey to find a place to produce the Comedy Hour from San Francisco for the 1970s.

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:10.140
We're thinking about moving the show up to San Francisco.

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:10.800
The whole show up to San Francisco.

00:43:10.920 --> 00:43:13.940
We feel it will be easier to work, more productive.

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:19.280
And I was looking at the Palace of Fine Arts in San Francisco and I got a call.

00:43:19.420 --> 00:43:21.960
I was talking to Mayor Aliotto who was going to bring the show up.

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:22.840
We're going to be subsidized.

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:24.300
There's going to be a San Francisco show.

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:26.100
Sort of like Jackie Gleason West.

00:43:26.260 --> 00:43:26.460
Yes.

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:27.620
Remember he was from Miami Beach.

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:29.980
And so I got a call, I think, in the mayor's office.

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:32.720
saying, from my attorney saying,

00:43:32.740 --> 00:43:35.590
don't get upset, but you've been fired.

00:43:35.690 --> 00:43:38.310
But don't say anything, maybe it'll go away.

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:41.830
So I didn't say anything for a day.

00:43:42.350 --> 00:43:44.910
And I believe it was on a Friday,

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:47.240
because they said, let it go through the weekend.

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:49.760
So what they did was fire us on a Friday,

00:43:49.880 --> 00:43:52.040
so I think it was difficult to get press.

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:53.180
I believe it was a Friday.

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:54.760
So that's how I found out.

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:56.980
And a lot of people called it a cancellation.

00:43:57.300 --> 00:43:58.160
Your show was canceled.

00:43:58.160 --> 00:43:59.900
But we at both Dick

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.620
and I feel strongly about saying that it was a firing.

00:44:03.390 --> 00:44:04.770
Both ways, you're off the air.

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:05.590
Both ways, you're off the air.

00:44:05.650 --> 00:44:08.290
No one in the history of television, no show, has ever been fired like that.

00:44:08.490 --> 00:44:12.830
And the way we talk about firing, shows don't last forever, let's face it.

00:44:13.250 --> 00:44:16.150
Cancellation is the normal process of taking a show off the air.

00:44:16.450 --> 00:44:17.710
You don't renew a contract.

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:22.230
And firing was, we were in the midst of a contract, and they cut us off.

00:44:22.810 --> 00:44:23.650
We heard about it.

00:44:23.670 --> 00:44:24.710
I heard about it from Tommy.

00:44:25.510 --> 00:44:27.970
But his attitude was at the time, don't worry.

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:29.350
I mean, this is just temporary.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.900
because we felt it was impossible for them to cancel us

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:34.580
because we were so hot.

00:44:35.340 --> 00:44:36.920
That's very stupid thinking, by the way,

00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:38.080
in case anyone wants to know.

00:44:38.830 --> 00:44:39.910
Anybody can be canceled,

00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:41.830
and anybody can be replaced in 10 minutes.

00:44:42.010 --> 00:44:43.550
The network picked up the show,

00:44:44.460 --> 00:44:46.120
renewed it, formally notified us,

00:44:46.540 --> 00:44:47.760
you're coming back in the fall,

00:44:47.800 --> 00:44:48.960
and then they canceled it.

00:44:50.980 --> 00:44:55.160
And in the legal dispute over their right to cancel it,

00:44:55.770 --> 00:44:57.190
the Smothers Brothers prevailed.

00:44:57.570 --> 00:44:59.890
They had not violated the terms of their contract.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:04.950
They had not done anything wrong, so that they were owed for a season of shows.

00:45:05.840 --> 00:45:10.690
Of course, the network chose to pay that contract off rather than actually buy the shows and

00:45:10.690 --> 00:45:11.610
put us on the air.

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:16.080
God forbid they should put us on the air after an Emmy-winning season.

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:24.190
And I think the cancellation was already in the air when we got our Emmy.

00:45:24.290 --> 00:45:28.780
We went up there and received an Emmy knowing that the statue was the last souvenir we would

00:45:28.780 --> 00:45:29.960
have of that particular show.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.560
that we were not going to be back in the fall.

00:45:32.070 --> 00:45:35.530
It became clear at that point

00:45:35.530 --> 00:45:40.120
that things just started getting uptight and tense,

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:43.600
and more people started coming to watch rehearsals,

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:47.320
and more material was looked at and changed,

00:45:47.360 --> 00:45:51.700
and it was like a real push-or-shove kind of issue,

00:45:51.740 --> 00:45:56.760
like, wait a minute, how do you function in this context now?

00:45:56.880 --> 00:45:58.300
You can't do what you're doing.

00:45:58.540 --> 00:45:59.840
And it would get pretty interesting.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.660
in the dressing room, I'll tell you, because Tom and Dick didn't take this lying down.

00:46:03.960 --> 00:46:08.620
And you'd have this exchange between Tom and Dick and Ken Fritz, my partner, and myself

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:14.900
in the dressing room, in which Tommy would finally say, really at the top of his lungs,

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:18.440
you tell CBS to take this show and shove it, you know?

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:26.720
I think because he challenged CBS and the confrontation was straight on, full on challenge,

00:46:26.960 --> 00:46:29.500
he didn't go quietly into that good night.

00:46:30.030 --> 00:46:33.850
In fact, when he knew they were going to drop him, he just kind of turned the knife.

00:46:34.150 --> 00:46:36.230
And we watched and we thought, is this wise?

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:38.270
I think it was the wise thing to do.

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:39.690
He did not go quietly.

00:46:40.370 --> 00:46:46.290
I feel that the Nixon presidency certainly contributed to the demise of this Mothers Brothers show

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:52.550
because we were very critical of the government in general, even when Johnson was there.

00:46:53.250 --> 00:46:58.530
We were critical of the Vietnam War and critical of all the things that really American youth were critical of at that time.

00:46:58.530 --> 00:46:58.650
time.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.480
The election of Nixon, I think, had a lot to do with it.

00:47:02.560 --> 00:47:06.740
We were told at the time, and I don't know the validity of this, but that Mr. Stanton,

00:47:06.860 --> 00:47:13.810
who was at the network at that time, was a very strong Nixon supporter and was constantly

00:47:13.810 --> 00:47:20.950
embarrassed by the fact that on his network there was a show that was satirizing the presidency

00:47:20.950 --> 00:47:24.470
and the vice presidency and other aspects of the government.

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:29.920
Talking about this firing of the Smothers Brothers show, a lot of people out there

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:33.040
There are a number of people who said they were right and good, just deserved to get

00:47:33.040 --> 00:47:33.380
fired.

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:38.860
Well, anyway, it was not good for our career to be fired, it sort of cut it off, so we had

00:47:38.860 --> 00:47:39.580
to sue CBS.

00:47:39.840 --> 00:47:45.020
And a long story short, we sued them, it took about three years to go to court, and the Smothers

00:47:45.020 --> 00:47:46.280
Brothers prevailed.

00:47:47.000 --> 00:47:52.080
CBS got, we sued them for a breach of contract, copyright infringement, stuff like that.

00:47:52.280 --> 00:47:52.940
And we won.

00:47:53.040 --> 00:47:55.000
We maybe lost the war, but we won that battle.

00:47:55.240 --> 00:47:59.200
And it was important for the comedy hour to win and go to court, because it was an

00:47:59.200 --> 00:47:59.940
an emotional thing.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:03.980
The war was the reason, basically, the show was, we were fired.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:07.220
Basically, it was the Vietnam War, our continuation in it,

00:48:07.300 --> 00:48:11.330
and people now, in hindsight, say, why were you fired for saying bad words?

00:48:11.410 --> 00:48:12.270
It was never bad words.

00:48:12.370 --> 00:48:16.550
It was some thoughts that were very, very, very passionate

00:48:16.550 --> 00:48:18.290
and presented very strongly,

00:48:18.570 --> 00:48:21.760
even though the show that you just saw had nothing about.

00:48:22.090 --> 00:48:23.630
Yeah, I think it's pretty apparent.

00:48:24.660 --> 00:48:29.320
Nothing about the war or Vietnam, but the attitude was strong.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:35.660
was gone, and now we get to see him again, another little trip back into space.

00:48:36.920 --> 00:48:40.900
Well, I hope this comedy hour brought back some pleasant memories for you all, I know

00:48:40.900 --> 00:48:45.090
you had a lot of pleasant memories there, memory lane, and for those of you who weren't

00:48:45.090 --> 00:48:48.010
around back then, we hope maybe we showed you a little bit about what the world in TV

00:48:48.010 --> 00:48:49.550
were like in 1969.
